# Cats developer workspace

Generated from `cats-one/templates/workspace/AGENTS.md.template`, the workspace
manifest and member skill inventory. Edit those tracked sources to share changes
with other development machines.

Refresh both agents and check the result from this parent directory:

| OS | Command (no parameters needed) |
| --- | --- |
| Windows | `.\cats-one\scripts\windows\Sync-WorkspaceSkills.ps1` |
| macOS | `./cats-one/scripts/macos/sync-workspace-skills.sh` |
| Linux | `./cats-one/scripts/linux/sync-workspace-skills.sh` |

Add `-WhatIf` on Windows or `--dry-run` on Bash to preview. The explicit
`cats-one/scripts/workspace.mjs` interface supports individual agent targets;
see `cats-one/docs/setup-guide.md`.

## Choose the owning repository

{{MEMBERS}}

Before changing a member, read its `AGENTS.md`, your own agent-specific file when
present (for example, `CODEX.md` for Codex), and relevant member guides. Read only
your own agent-specific file. Apply each member's rules within its scope; a task
spanning repositories can require reading several members' instructions.

Run repository-relative commands with the owning checkout as the working
directory. Each checkout has independent Git state; this parent need not be a
repository. Runtime owns generic product workspace capabilities; platform owns
Desktop/App selection and installation; apps owns utility artifact production;
plugins owns plugin producer recipes and packaged plugin artifacts.

## Version and upgrade policy

For every member, preserve compatibility within a `0.x` minor line; breaking
public APIs, CLI/config contracts or persisted-data requirements move to the next
minor. Stable `1.x+` breaking contracts require a major bump. Data-format changes
must include a tested upgrade path with validation, backup, atomic replacement
and failure recovery; release notes and version bumps alone are insufficient.
One-time data migrations are permitted without retaining obsolete execution APIs.
Follow `cats-one/docs/release-guide.md` and each member's release SOP. Record the
needed boundary now; bump or publish only within the user's release authorization.

## Release preparation and completion

For all members and agents, prepare release notes, version fields, exact source
and artifact pins, compatibility/migration notes and signing policy in the
original bump commit/PR. After publication, run required verification, report
the results in the final response, and stop; Actions run results/logs and release
assets are the hosted evidence. Do not edit a published GitHub Release, create
post-release tracking files, status-only commits or PRs, or offer any of these as
optional follow-ups. This rule overrides generic project-memory sync for routine
publication outcomes. Further repository edits need an explicit request or a
concrete release defect within authorized scope. After successful verification,
perform authorized cleanup and finish; do not chase unrelated main commits for
documentation. See cats-one/docs/release-guide.md#release-preparation-and-completion.

## Branches and worktrees

Several agents can work in these checkouts at once. Keep each member's main
checkout on its default branch and do the work in a worktree inside that member,
under its Git-ignored `.claude/worktrees/`, not in a sibling directory of this
parent, which folder-wide tools such as bulk `git pull` scripts also scan. When
you clean up after a merge, remove the worktree you created for it, then run the
member's merged-branch sweep from its main checkout; the sweep skips any branch a
worktree still holds. Remove only worktrees you created, and keep one past its
merge only when the user asks.

## Maintenance skills

{{SKILLS}}

Interpret a skill's repository-relative paths and commands in its owning member.
Skill-local resource links remain relative to the skill directory. Edit canonical
sources listed above, then rerun workspace sync after pulling changes.
Runtime's separate product `runtime-skills/` library is outside this developer inventory.

Repository-local sessions retain their own setup. Changing a shell command's
working directory does not reload the active conversation's skills. Reopen a
parent-root session when the agent host requires it to discover changed skills.
